# The Python compute pipes node, as a container-image Lambda.
#
# Built with the repository root as its context, as every other Dockerfile here
# is:
#
#   docker build --progress=plain \
#     --build-arg "JETS_VERSION=$JETS_VERSION" \
#     --build-arg "JETS_GIT_SHA=$JETS_GIT_SHA" \
#     -t cpipes_python_lambda:latest \
#     -f dockerfiles/Dockerfile.cpipes_python_lambda .
#
# **Why a container and not a zip.** A zip-packaged Lambda is capped at 250 MB
# unpacked, and the interpreter is not inside that budget on a zip deployment;
# the node's own closure -- pydantic, jsonschema, boto3 and a Postgres driver --
# does not fit beside one, and a site operator bringing pyarrow (~120 MB) would
# not come close. A container-image function is capped at 10 GB.
#
# **Measured 2026-09-18, and the partition is the argument rather than the
# total: 571.7 MB, of which the base image is 517.0 MB and everything this file
# adds is 54.7 MB** -- 46.2 MB of installed distributions, 8.3 MB of the two
# source trees and 0.3 MB of handler, checks and version stamp. So the
# interpreter base alone is 2.07x the zip cap, before a line of this node exists:
# the container is not a convenience taken to leave headroom, it is the only
# packaging in which the node fits. Against the container limit the image was 5.7%
# of 10 GB. The number that decides whether the lambda arm is *usable* is not
# this one but the per-invocation reference load, which is P9-T16's to measure
# (P9-I03, P9-R04).
#
# **Re-measured 2026-09-19 at P9-T07: 735.0 MB, and the whole of the 163.0 MB
# rise is pyarrow.** The install layer goes 46.2 MB -> 208 MB and no other layer
# moves; the image is 7.35% of the 10 GB limit where it was 5.7%. pyarrow is
# `cpipes_node`'s parquet device writer and is a dependency rather than an extra,
# because X3 asks for every table in CSV *and* Parquet by changing
# `device_writer_type` alone, and a format that works only where somebody
# remembered an extra makes an exit criterion a function of the install. The
# runtime check below was re-run on that image and is unchanged: INIT success, the
# event accepted, and the first thing missing still the AWS account.
#
# **A second measurement the same day, and it is a finding rather than a figure**
# (P9-I61): this file copies `tools/cpipes_node` **whole**, and the root
# `.dockerignore` did not exclude a virtual environment. On a checkout where
# anybody had run `uv run`, that COPY layer was **183 MB** of the developer's own
# wheels -- platform-native extension modules included -- and the image built to
# **916.8 MB**. It is this file's own argument about `jetsclient_ide/node_modules`
# one tree over, and it was invisible because the first measurement was taken on a
# clean checkout. `.dockerignore` now excludes `**/.venv/` and the tool caches,
# and the COPY layer is 1.4 MB.
#
# **It is the Dockerfile.cpipes_native_lambda set's pattern with one structural
# difference, and the difference is an absence worth stating.** That set is two
# files: a base image copying `bootstrap` out of a builder stage, and a `_ws`
# sibling adding the compiled workspace. There is no `_ws` sibling here, and it
# is measured rather than forgotten:
#
#   - the compiled workspace is read by the jetrules engine, and this node
#     declares no jetrules token at all (`cpipes-node scope`), so baking
#     `/workspaces/<ws>` in would assert a need that does not exist;
#   - the per-workspace content a Python node wants is the site's own operator
#     package and the module registering it, which is a pip requirement and a
#     handler module rather than a directory copied out of `compile_ws`.
#
# So the site half of the pattern is a site-authored Dockerfile deriving
# `FROM cpipes_python_lambda:latest`, installing its own distribution and
# shipping its own handler. **Writing that file before a site distribution
# exists would be a knob with no consumer**, which is the shape `I-820`'s own
# argument refuses. §20.4's rule -- a `_ws` image inherits the selection its
# parent made, and only the image naming the source needs the ARG -- holds here
# with the source being a pip requirement.
#
# **The handler is named at synth and not baked here.** `DockerImageCode_FromEcr`
# sets `Cmd` (`build_cpipes_lambdas.go`), exactly as it overrides the native
# image's to `bootstrap`, so which module a deployment runs is visible in the
# synthesised template. The CMD below is the default for a bare `docker run` of
# this image and for the Runtime Interface Emulator.
#
# **Exercised through the runtime, not only through the build.** The base image carries
# `aws-lambda-rie`, so the deployed entry is reachable on a workstation:
#
#   docker run -d -p 9321:8080 -e JETS_BUCKET=x -e JETS_REGION=us-east-1 \
#     -e JETS_DSN_SECRET=x cpipes_python_lambda:latest handler.lambda_handler
#   curl -XPOST localhost:9321/2015-03-31/functions/function/invocations \
#     -d '{"id":0,"jp":"","pe":1}'
#
# Measured 2026-09-18: `INIT RTDONE(status: success)` -- so `Settings.from_env` and the `Node`
# composed at import -- then the event reaching `lambda_handler`, `NodeArgs(**event)` accepting
# the three fields, and the invocation failing at `get_secret_value` with `NoCredentialsError`
# on a machine that has no AWS. That is the whole chain from the runtime interface into the
# package, and the first thing missing is the account rather than anything in the image.
#
# **What this image is not: reproducible.** Criterion 98's instrument for the Go
# images is a rebuild that is a total cache hit writing the same image id, and
# that claim rests on `go.sum`. There is no lock file for these two Python
# distributions, so `pip install` resolves pydantic, jsonschema, boto3 and
# psycopg afresh at every cold build and two builds a month apart are not the
# same image. The mitigation here is disclosure rather than a pin: `pip freeze`
# is written into /VERSION.txt, so a deployed image can be asked what it
# contains. A constraints file is the repair and it belongs with the packaging
# rather than with the image (P9-I53).

FROM public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.12

# JETS_VERSION is the build's timestamp, stamped identically into every image of
# a build run, and JETS_GIT_SHA is the branch and commit. Same shape and same
# file as Dockerfile.cpipes_native_lambda writes, so
# `docker run --entrypoint cat <img> /VERSION.txt` answers the same question
# about either image.
ARG JETS_VERSION
ENV JETS_VERSION=${JETS_VERSION}
ARG JETS_GIT_SHA
ENV JETS_GIT_SHA=${JETS_GIT_SHA}
RUN echo "----------------JetStore-Version------------------" >> /VERSION.txt && \
    echo "JETS_VERSION: ${JETS_VERSION}"                      >> /VERSION.txt && \
    echo "JETS_GIT_SHA: ${JETS_GIT_SHA}"                      >> /VERSION.txt && \
    echo "----------------*********************-------------" >> /VERSION.txt

# The two source trees, copied whole.
#
# **`cpipes_contract` ships as a tree and not as a wheel alone, and that is
# P9-I27 rather than a preference.** `cpipes_model.py` -- the Pydantic model that
# is the source of truth for the whole `.pc.json` -- is a *sibling file* of the
# `cpipes_contract` package, and that package's wheel declares
# `packages = ["cpipes_contract"]`, so an installed wheel does not carry the
# model at all. `cpipes_contract.reflect` loads it through
# `importlib.util.spec_from_file_location` and so does `cpipes_node/contract.py`,
# whose second searched path is `<site-packages>/cpipes_model.py`. The `cp` below
# puts it exactly there, which also makes it a plain importable module, and
# `image_check.py` asserts both.
#
# **The repair belongs on the other side of the seam**: moving the file into its
# package costs one default path in `cpipes_contract/main.py` and deletes this
# copy, the four `type: ignore[valid-type]` comments in `contract.py` and that
# module's own retiring guard. Until then this is what makes the node able to
# read a `.pc.json` at all.
COPY tools/cpipes_contract /src/cpipes_contract
COPY tools/cpipes_node     /src/cpipes_node

# psycopg is the image's, not the package's. `cpipes_node` imports no database
# driver by design (`awslambda.Node.connect`), and the default handler this image
# ships is where the Go main's `dbc.NewDbConnection` lands.
#
# boto3 is in the Lambda Python base image already; `cpipes_node[s3]` names it
# anyway, so the dependency is declared where it is used rather than inherited
# from a base image that could drop it.
RUN python -m pip install --no-cache-dir --upgrade pip \
    && python -m pip install --no-cache-dir \
        /src/cpipes_contract \
        "/src/cpipes_node[s3]" \
        "psycopg[binary]>=3.1" \
    && SITE="$(python -c 'import cpipes_contract, pathlib; print(pathlib.Path(cpipes_contract.__file__).resolve().parent.parent)')" \
    && cp /src/cpipes_contract/cpipes_model.py "$SITE/cpipes_model.py" \
    && echo "----------------Python-distributions--------------" >> /VERSION.txt \
    && python -m pip freeze                                      >> /VERSION.txt \
    && echo "----------------*********************-------------" >> /VERSION.txt

COPY dockerfiles/cpipes_node_lambda/handler.py     ${LAMBDA_TASK_ROOT}/handler.py
COPY dockerfiles/cpipes_node_lambda/image_check.py /image_check.py

# The build-time checks. See image_check.py for what each one is for; the three
# variables are `cpipes_node.settings.REQUIRED`, with values that are obviously
# not a deployment's.
RUN JETS_BUCKET=layout-check JETS_REGION=layout-check JETS_DSN_SECRET=layout-check \
    python /image_check.py

# **`/src` is kept rather than removed, because removing it buys nothing.** A
# `RUN rm -rf /src` deletes the files in a new layer and leaves the two COPY
# layers exactly where they were, so the line would cost a layer and reclaim no
# bytes while reading as if it had. What the trees earn in place is P9-I27's
# requirement read literally: a reader of a deployed image can see the model file
# the node loads and the sibling-file layout that makes the `cp` necessary.

CMD [ "handler.lambda_handler" ]
